WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.170
Take the sentence: the student put the

2
00:00:03.170 --> 00:00:05.870
laptop in the bag because it was full.

3
00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:07.620
What does it refer to?

4
00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:09.400
People answer instantly.

5
00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:12.290
Older language models often

6
00:00:12.290 --> 00:00:13.270
guessed the laptop.

7
00:00:14.590 --> 00:00:17.150
Today: scaled dot-product attention,

8
00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:19.750
why it is divided by the square root

9
00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:22.690
of d, and how to teach the transformer

10
00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:24.210
without drowning students.

11
00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:28.000
Attention lets every token build its

12
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.300
new representation as a weighted

13
00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:32.200
average of all the tokens.

14
00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:34.960
The weights say how relevant

15
00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:36.340
each other token is.

16
00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:39.120
So it can look straight at the bag.

17
00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:42.350
Each token makes three vectors.

18
00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:46.040
Its query asks what it needs, each

19
00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:48.520
key says what a token offers, and

20
00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:50.040
values carry the content.

21
00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:52.790
Scores are queries times keys,

22
00:00:53.290 --> 00:00:55.970
divided by root d, then softmax.

23
00:00:57.290 --> 00:00:59.270
Here are one head's attention

24
00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:01.490
weights from it to the other nouns.

25
00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:03.850
Most of the weight goes to bag.

26
00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:06.610
The student and the laptop get little.

27
00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:10.090
If query and key entries have variance

28
00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:13.410
one, their dot product has variance d.

29
00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:16.570
Large scores push softmax into

30
00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:18.930
saturation, where gradients vanish.

31
00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:21.650
Dividing by root d keeps

32
00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:22.990
the variance near one.

33
00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:27.480
Vaswani and colleagues' diagram has too

34
00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:29.300
much in it for a first lecture.

35
00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:33.450
Start with one head, three

36
00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:36.330
tokens and two dimensions.

37
00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:41.100
Students compute the scores by hand, then

38
00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:44.480
you add heads, layers and positions.

39
00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:46.420
Quick check!

40
00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:50.660
The key dimension d is sixty four.

41
00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:52.920
By what number do we divide

42
00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:54.640
the raw attention scores?

43
00:01:56.050 --> 00:01:58.510
Eight, the square root of sixty four.

44
00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:01.500
Without it, scores would have a standard

45
00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:04.320
deviation near eight and softmax would

46
00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.200
almost always pick one token.

47
00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:09.960
In the app, open the attention lab.

48
00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:13.100
Type a sentence, pick a word, and

49
00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:14.820
see which words it attends to.

50
00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:16.910
Change the sentence and

51
00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:18.110
watch the weights move.

52
00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:20.150
Your turn.

53
00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:23.390
Attention alone treats

54
00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:25.270
its inputs as a set.

55
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:28.000
What does the transformer add

56
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.420
so word order matters?

57
00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:33.370
Positional encodings.

58
00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:36.390
Remember: attention is a weighted

59
00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:39.670
average, scores are queries times keys

60
00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:43.570
over root d, and position must be added.

61
00:02:44.770 --> 00:02:47.550
Try it in your next lecture: give pairs

62
00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:50.450
three two-dimensional query and key

63
00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:50.770
vectors.

64
00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:53.880
They compute the scores, the softmax

65
00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:57.000
and the output by hand, then compare

66
00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:58.720
with three lines of NumPy.
